- id: TF-3D3939228B
  title: Container runs in privileged mode
  category: Elevation of Privilege
  element: k8s:Container:shop/storefront/web
  severity: critical
  description: 'Container shop/web runs with `privileged: true`, which disables essentially every container
    isolation control: all Linux capabilities are granted, /dev is fully exposed, and AppArmor/seccomp
    are effectively bypassed. An attacker with code execution in this container can mount the host filesystem,
    write to /etc/kubernetes, read the kubelet credentials, and take over the node -- and from the node,
    every other pod scheduled on it.'
  evidence:
  - description: securityContext.privileged is true
    observed: true
    location: k8s/app.yaml:23
  risk:
    score: 25
    likelihood: 5
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: 3
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-250
    - CWE-269
    mitre:
    - T1611
    - T1610
    cis:
    - 5.2.1
    nist:
    - AC-6
    - CM-7
    owasp:
    - K01
  rule_id: TF-K8S-001
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Remove privileged mode and grant only the specific capabilities needed.
    effort: medium
- id: TF-A8295E0EEA
  title: Container mounts the container runtime socket
  category: Elevation of Privilege
  element: k8s:Deployment:shop/storefront
  severity: critical
  description: 'Deployment shop/storefront mounts the Docker/containerd/CRI-O socket. This is equivalent
    to granting root on the node: anyone who can write to the socket can start a new privileged container
    with the host filesystem mounted. There is no meaningful sandbox left.'
  evidence:
  - description: hostPath volume includes the runtime socket
    observed:
    - /var/run/docker.sock
    location: k8s/app.yaml:7
  risk:
    score: 25
    likelihood: 5
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: 2
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-250
    - CWE-284
    mitre:
    - T1610
    - T1611
    cis:
    - 5.2.4
    nist:
    - AC-6
    - SC-7
  rule_id: TF-K8S-004
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Remove the socket mount; use a rootless build tool or a proxy with an allowlist.
    effort: high
- id: TF-E9E02CF5E9
  title: Plaintext credential in container environment variables
  category: Information Disclosure
  element: k8s:Container:shop/storefront/web
  severity: critical
  description: Environment variables DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY on Container shop/web contain literal
    values and are named like credentials. Env vars are visible to anyone with `kubectl describe pod`,
    are captured in crash dumps and in most APM/error-tracking agents, are inherited by every child process,
    and are readable from /proc/<pid>/environ by anything sharing the PID namespace.
  evidence:
  - description: 'env vars with literal values: DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY'
    observed:
    - DB_PASSWORD
    - STRIPE_API_KEY
    location: k8s/app.yaml:23
  risk:
    score: 25
    likelihood: 5
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: 3
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-798
    - CWE-522
    mitre:
    - T1552.001
    - T1552.007
    cis:
    - 5.4.1
    nist:
    - IA-5
    - SC-28
    owasp:
    - K08
  rule_id: TF-K8S-015
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Move the value into a Secret and reference it, or mount it as a file.
    effort: low
- id: TF-40054F05CC
  title: Ingress does not enforce TLS
  category: Information Disclosure
  element: k8s:Ingress:shop/storefront-ingress
  severity: critical
  description: Ingress shop/storefront-ingress accepts traffic without TLS. Credentials, session cookies,
    and response data cross the public internet in cleartext, and there is nothing to stop an on-path
    attacker rewriting responses to serve malicious JavaScript.
