    - SC-28
  rule_id: TF-RBAC-005
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Remove write access; provision Secrets through a controller instead.
    effort: medium
- id: TF-54F847345C
  title: Compute instance permits IMDSv1
  category: Elevation of Privilege
  element: tf:aws_instance.worker
  severity: medium
  description: aws_instance worker allows IMDSv1. An SSRF in any application on the instance reaches 169.254.169.254
    with a single unauthenticated GET and returns the instance role's temporary credentials. IMDSv2's
    PUT-token requirement blocks the entire SSRF class.
  evidence:
  - description: http_tokens is not 'required'
    observed: false
    location: tf/main.tf:24
  risk:
    score: 10
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-918
    - CWE-522
    mitre:
    - T1552.005
    nist:
    - SC-7
    - IA-5
  rule_id: TF-CLOUD-009
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Require IMDSv2 and lower the hop limit.
    effort: low
- id: TF-79E48151A6
  title: Image runs as root
  category: Elevation of Privilege
  element: docker:image:Dockerfile#node:latest
  severity: medium
  description: The final stage of DockerImage node:latest has no USER instruction, so the image runs as
    UID 0. Every downstream consumer inherits that default -- an orchestrator has to opt out explicitly,
    and most manifests never do.
  evidence:
  - description: no USER instruction in the final stage
    observed: null
    location: Dockerfile:1
  risk:
    score: 10
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-250
    mitre:
    - T1610
    cis:
    - '4.1'
    nist:
    - AC-6
  rule_id: TF-DKR-001
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Create and switch to a non-root user.
    effort: medium
- id: TF-34D8EACA5D
  title: Build pipes remote script directly into a shell
  category: Tampering
  element: docker:image:Dockerfile#node:latest
  severity: medium
  description: A `curl ... | sh` pattern executes unverified remote code as root during the build. Whoever
    controls that URL controls the contents of your image, and the compromise leaves no trace in the Dockerfile.
  evidence:
  - description: curl/wget piped to a shell
    observed:
    - value: curl -fsSL https://get.example.com/install.sh | sh
      line: 3
    location: Dockerfile:3
  risk:
    score: 10
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 5
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-494
    mitre:
    - T1195.002
    - T1059
    nist:
    - SI-7
    - CM-7
  rule_id: TF-DKR-005
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Download, verify the checksum, inspect, then execute.
    effort: low
