  rule_id: TF-RBAC-007
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Disable automount on the default service account.
    effort: low
- id: TF-BD31E364C4
  title: Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0
  category: Spoofing
  element: tf:aws_security_group.web
  severity: medium
  description: aws_security_group web permits inbound traffic from the entire internet on port(s) 22.
    If any of those are 22, 3389, 3306, 5432, or 6379 this is immediately exploitable; even for 443 it
    removes the option of IP-based containment during an incident.
  evidence:
  - description: open ports = 22 from 0.0.0.0/0
    observed:
    - 22
    location: tf/main.tf:14
  risk:
    score: 8
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-284
    mitre:
    - T1190
    - T1133
    nist:
    - SC-7
    - AC-4
  rule_id: TF-CLOUD-003
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Restrict to known CIDRs, or front the service with a load balancer and WAF.
    effort: low
- id: TF-CAB2B8A169
  title: Data store has encryption at rest disabled
  category: Information Disclosure
  element: tf:aws_db_instance.shop
  severity: medium
  description: aws_db_instance shop stores data unencrypted at rest. Snapshot sharing, disk decommissioning,
    and support access all become disclosure paths, and most compliance regimes treat this as a reportable
    control failure on its own.
  evidence:
  - description: encryption is explicitly disabled
    observed: false
    location: tf/main.tf:5
  risk:
    score: 8
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-311
    mitre:
    - T1530
    nist:
    - SC-28
  rule_id: TF-CLOUD-004
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Enable encryption with a customer-managed KMS key.
    effort: medium
- id: TF-207E7BBEE5
  title: Compose service uses host networking
  category: Spoofing
  element: compose:service:cache
  severity: medium
  description: 'network_mode host removes network namespace isolation: the container can bind any host
    port and sniff all host traffic.'
  evidence:
  - description: 'network_mode: host'
    observed: true
    location: docker-compose.yml:11
  risk:
    score: 8
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-668
    mitre:
    - T1040
    nist:
    - SC-7
  rule_id: TF-CMP-006
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Use a user-defined bridge network and publish only required ports.
    effort: medium
- id: TF-923D4614F5
  title: Credential-like values stored in a ConfigMap
  category: Information Disclosure
  element: k8s:ConfigMap:shop/shop-config
  severity: medium
  description: 'ConfigMap shop/shop-config holds keys API_TOKEN that look like credentials. ConfigMaps
    get none of the (already modest) protections Secrets receive: they are not candidates for encryption
    at rest by default, are routinely granted broad read access, and are frequently dumped wholesale into
    logs and support bundles.'
  evidence:
  - description: 'credential-like keys: API_TOKEN'
    observed:
    - API_TOKEN
    location: k8s/app.yaml:90
  risk:
    score: 8
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-522
    - CWE-312
    mitre:
    - T1552.001
    nist:
    - SC-28
    - IA-5
    owasp:
    - K08
  rule_id: TF-DATA-002
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Move these keys into a Secret or an external secret store, and rotate them.
    effort: low
- id: TF-ABBB1D0FE8
  title: Container image is not pinned by digest
  category: Tampering
  element: k8s:Container:shop/postgres/postgres
  severity: medium
  description: Container shop/postgres uses a version tag rather than a digest. Version tags are still
    mutable in most registries, so this is weaker than it looks, though far better than :latest.
  evidence:
  - description: image = postgres:15
    observed: postgres:15
    location: k8s/app.yaml:115
  risk:
    score: 6
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 3
    exposure_hops: 3
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-829
    mitre:
    - T1195.002
    nist:
    - CM-2
  rule_id: TF-K8S-012
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Resolve the tag to a digest in CI and commit the digest.
    effort: low
- id: TF-F32A911991
  title: Workload has no liveness or readiness probe
  category: Denial of Service
  element: k8s:Container:shop/postgres/postgres
  severity: medium
  description: Without probes, Kubernetes cannot tell a hung process from a healthy one. A deadlocked
    or resource-starved container keeps receiving traffic, which turns a partial failure into a user-visible
    outage and masks the early signal of an ongoing resource-exhaustion attack.
  evidence:
  - description: no livenessProbe
    observed: true
    location: k8s/app.yaml:115
  - description: no readinessProbe
    observed: true
    location: k8s/app.yaml:115
  risk:
    score: 6
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 3
    exposure_hops: 3
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-703
    mitre:
    - T1499
    nist:
    - SI-4
  rule_id: TF-K8S-019
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Add liveness and readiness probes.
    effort: low
- id: TF-07502178B5
  title: Single-replica workload is a single point of failure
  category: Denial of Service
  element: k8s:StatefulSet:shop/postgres
  severity: medium
  description: StatefulSet shop/postgres is internet-reachable but runs a single replica, so any crash,
    eviction, or node drain takes the service down. It also means a single successful resource-exhaustion
    request has full impact.
  evidence:
  - description: spec.replicas = 1
    observed: 1
    location: k8s/app.yaml:99
  risk:
    score: 6
    likelihood: 2
    impact: 3
    exposure_hops: 2
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-1188
    mitre:
    - T1499
    nist:
    - CP-2
  rule_id: TF-K8S-020
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Run at least two replicas with a PodDisruptionBudget and anti-affinity.
    effort: low
- id: TF-5A5C669706
  title: Base image uses a mutable tag
  category: Tampering
  element: docker:image:Dockerfile#node:latest
  severity: low
  description: DockerImage node:latest builds FROM `node:latest`. Two builds of the same commit can produce
    different images, which breaks reproducibility and makes it impossible to say with confidence what
    is running in production.
  evidence:
  - description: FROM node:latest
    observed: node:latest
    location: Dockerfile:1
  risk:
    score: 4
    likelihood: 1
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-829
    - CWE-494
    mitre:
    - T1195.002
    cis:
    - '4.2'
    nist:
    - CM-2
    - SI-7
  rule_id: TF-DKR-002
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Pin the base image by digest.
    effort: low
- id: TF-78D16B78D0
  title: Remote content fetched with ADD
  category: Tampering
  element: docker:image:Dockerfile#node:latest
  severity: low
  description: ADD with a URL downloads and unpacks remote content with no integrity check. If the upstream
    host is compromised or DNS is hijacked, arbitrary content lands in the image at build time -- a textbook
    supply chain injection point.
  evidence:
  - description: ADD from a remote URL
    observed:
    - value: https://example.com/tool.tar.gz /opt/tool.tar.gz
      line: 4
    location: Dockerfile:4
  risk:
    score: 4
    likelihood: 1
    impact: 4
    exposure_hops: null
  references:
    cwe:
    - CWE-494
    - CWE-829
    mitre:
    - T1195.002
    - T1105
    cis:
    - '4.9'
    nist:
    - SI-7
  rule_id: TF-DKR-004
  mitigation:
    status: open
    description: Download explicitly and verify a checksum or signature.
    effort: low
